※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4531

※ A function answering with a reference says how long what it names lives:
※ `static` in the type, or `from` naming the parameter it was borrowed from.
※ Neither of those can be written about a reference held inside something else.
※ `static` belongs to a reference and a tuple may hold several; `from` speaks
※ for the whole answer and there would be nothing to attach it to.  So what is
※ refused here is not the reference but the wrapping, and the way to write this
※ is to answer with the reference itself.
※
※ Rust does write it, because a lifetime there is a name the signature invents
※ and can be put on each reference of a compound separately.  The price is that
※ name at every signature; the language here answers with one reference and
※ needs no name at all.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6

fn pair(a: &u8) → 〈&u8, u8〉:
    〈a, 1u8〉
